// Memory, layout, and conversion intrinsics.
//
// Every declaration here is a **bodyless `#intrinsic` function** (§6.4, §9): an
// ordinary declaration with an ordinary signature, whose body the compiler
// supplies. There is no `$name` form and no second namespace for
// compiler-provided functions — a reader who wants to know what `cast` takes and
// returns reads it here, beside everything else core declares.
//
// `#intrinsic` is the mirror of `#lang`, and the direction is what keeps them
// apart: `#lang("add")` is the compiler looking *up* an item core provides;
// `#intrinsic("cast")` is core declaring an item the compiler *fills in*. The
// tag, not the name, is the identity — so renaming a declaration here is a
// library decision, not a compiler change.

// The sole explicit conversion (§6.5). `cast.<T>(x)` names the target; `cast(x)`
// takes it from context, which is ordinary return-position inference — `T` is
// the *first* parameter precisely so a turbofish can supply it and leave `U` to
// be inferred from the argument.
//
// What a cast promises depends on who wrote it: a `cast` the program wrote may
// lose precision (narrowing keeps the low bits, as at run time), while one the
// compiler inserted to settle a literal must be exact (§6.5).
@public cast :: #intrinsic("cast") func <T, U> (x: U) -> T

// Reinterpret the bits of `x` as a `T` of the same size. The size equality is a
// check the compiler makes at the call, not something the signature says — there
// is no bound that means "same layout".
@public transmute :: #intrinsic("transmute") func <T, U> (x: U) -> T

// Layout queries. Both are compile-time constants once `T` is concrete, which is
// why they may appear on the RHS of a `::`.
@public size_of  :: #intrinsic("size_of") func <T> () -> usize
@public align_of :: #intrinsic("align_of") func <T> () -> usize

// Allocation (§6.9).
//
// `new.<T>()` yields one zeroed, GC-managed `T`.
@public new :: #intrinsic("new") func <T> () -> *mut T

// `zeroed.<T>()` is a `T` whose every byte is zero, where it is written — a
// local, with no allocation. It is `new`'s contents without the heap: a value
// that is filled in member by member afterwards (`std/di`'s `invoke` builds a
// closure's arguments this way).
@public zeroed :: #intrinsic("zeroed") func <T> () -> T

// `make.<[]T>(len)` yields a zeroed, GC-managed slice of `len` elements.
//
// This is one of the two intrinsics whose result the signature cannot state: the
// type argument is written `[]T` and the result is `[]mut T` — the *mutability*
// is what the allocation adds, and no bound expresses that. The compiler applies
// it at the call site; the `[]T` written here is the shape, not the promise.
@public make :: #intrinsic("make") func <T> (len: usize) -> T

// Free `p`'s object **now**, rather than when the collector next runs (§6.9).
//
// The language is collected, so this is never required and is not how memory is
// normally released. It exists because the compiler already does exactly this
// wherever escape analysis proves an object cannot outlive its scope
// (`design/lir.md` §5), and a program that knows something the analysis cannot
// see — that a buffer is finished with well before its scope ends, say — has no
// other way to say so.
//
// Writing it takes the question on. The compiler stops inserting its own drop
// for that value, and **using the value afterwards is a compile error**: a
// pointer whose object was freed is the one thing the collector's whole point is
// to make impossible, so it is not left to a run-time surprise. What that check
// can see is one function's worth of a local's own name — an alias made before
// the drop is not tracked, and freeing through one is a way to get a dangling
// pointer past it.
//
// It takes `*mut T` because freeing an object is the most destructive write
// there is, and the permission to do it belongs in the type.
@public drop :: #intrinsic("drop") func <T> (p: *mut T) -> void

// Splice a file's bytes into the binary as a compile-time `[]u8`.
@public embed_file :: #intrinsic("embed_file") func (path: str) -> []u8

// Copy `src` onto the front of `dst`, as one block move.
//
// `src.len()` elements are copied, so `dst` must be at least that long; a
// caller that cannot promise it wants `std/mem`'s `copy`, which clamps to the
// shorter of the two and says how much it moved.
//
// **The two must not overlap.** Nothing here can check that — a slice knows its
// address and its length, and two slices into one buffer are two ordinary
// values — so it is stated rather than enforced, exactly as C states it.
//
// It takes slices and not a pointer and a count: the length is already part of
// the value, so the byte count is derived rather than trusted, and the
// mismatched-count mistake cannot be written.
@public copy_nonoverlapping :: #intrinsic("memcpy") func <T> (dst: []mut T, src: []T) -> void

// Set every byte of `dst` to `byte`.
//
// Bytes, not elements: this is the machine's fill, and what it writes is a byte
// pattern. `set_bytes(xs, 0)` zeroes any slice whatever its element type, which
// is what it is for; filling with an *element* is `std/mem`'s `fill`, which is
// a loop because a general `T` is not one byte.
@public set_bytes :: #intrinsic("memset") func <T> (dst: []mut T, byte: u8) -> void

tests :: #when(test) namespace {
  { assert } :: import "../rt/fail.nest"

  Mixed :: struct { n: i32, x: f64, on: bool, p: *i32, pair: (u8, i64) }

  @test
  zeroed_is_all_zero :: func () {
    let m: Mixed := zeroed.<Mixed>()
    assert(m.n == 0 && m.x == 0.0 && m.on == false)
    assert(m.pair.0 == 0 && m.pair.1 == 0)
    m.n = 4
    assert(m.n == 4, "a zeroed local is an ordinary one")
    assert(zeroed.<u64>() == 0)
  }
}
